# Landing page content. Each entry: slug -> dict(title, desc, h1, lede, body, faq)
L = {}

L['meditation-for-beginners'] = dict(
title="Meditation for Beginners: A Simple Step-by-Step Guide | Agni Institute",
desc="New to meditation? A clear, step-by-step beginner's guide to posture, breath, attention and building a daily practice, from the Agni Institute.",
h1="Meditation for Beginners",
lede="A clear, practical way to start sitting: posture, breath, attention, and how to make a few quiet minutes a day into a lasting habit.",
body="""
<h2>Why start meditating?</h2>
<p>Meditation is the training of attention. In its simplest form you sit still, choose something to rest your attention on, notice when the mind wanders, and gently bring it back. That small cycle, repeated over and over, is the whole exercise. Over time it tends to make the mind steadier, quieter and more observant.</p>
<p>At the Agni Institute, meditation is taught and practiced as the principal skill for investigating the nature of consciousness. Whether your interest is stress relief, clearer thinking, or a deeper inquiry into who and what we are, the first steps are the same, and they are simpler than many people expect.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Choose a time and a place</h2>
<p>Pick a time you can repeat every day. Many people find early morning easiest, before the day's demands start, but any regular time works. Choose a quiet spot where you are unlikely to be interrupted. It does not need to be special; a chair in a corner is enough. Consistency of time and place helps the habit form.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Sit comfortably and upright</h2>
<p>You do not need to sit cross-legged on the floor. A firm chair is fine. Sit with your feet flat, your back upright but not rigid, and your hands resting on your thighs or in your lap. Let the shoulders drop and the jaw soften. The aim is a posture that is alert without strain, one you can hold for ten or twenty minutes without fidgeting. If you are lying down you are more likely to fall asleep, so sitting is usually better.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Rest your attention on the breath</h2>
<p>Close your eyes or let your gaze rest softly on the floor in front of you. Breathe naturally, without trying to control the breath. Notice where you feel it most clearly: the air at the nostrils, the rise and fall of the chest, or the movement of the belly. Let that sensation be your anchor. Some beginners find it helpful to count breaths silently from one to ten and then start again.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Notice wandering and return</h2>
<p>Within a few breaths the mind will wander: to plans, memories, sounds, an itch. This is not failure; it is what minds do, and noticing it is the moment of practice. When you realize you have drifted, note it lightly ("thinking") and return your attention to the breath. Do this without judgment, as many times as needed. Each return strengthens attention a little, much as each repetition strengthens a muscle.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Start small and be consistent</h2>
<p>Five to ten minutes a day is a good beginning. A short daily sitting is more valuable than an occasional long one. Use a gentle timer so you are not checking the clock. As sitting becomes familiar, extend the time gradually toward twenty or thirty minutes. Keep a brief note of each session if it helps: how long you sat and anything you noticed.</p>

<h2>Common obstacles</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>"I can't stop thinking."</strong> You are not trying to stop thoughts, only to notice them and return. A busy mind gives you more chances to practice.</li>
<li><strong>Restlessness or discomfort.</strong> Adjust your posture mindfully if needed. Mild restlessness usually settles with regular practice.</li>
<li><strong>Sleepiness.</strong> Sit more upright, open your eyes slightly, or meditate at a time when you are more alert.</li>
<li><strong>Doubt.</strong> Progress is subtle at first. Give a regular practice several weeks before judging it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bringing practice into daily life</h2>
<p>Meditation is not only what happens on the cushion. Moments of attention can be brought into ordinary activity: a few conscious breaths before answering an email, full attention while walking or eating, noticing irritation as it arises rather than being carried away by it. In the lifestyle promoted by Agni Yoga, this steady cultivation of awareness and goodwill in daily life is as important as formal sitting.</p>

<h2>Learning with others</h2>
<p>Many people find it easier to establish a practice with guidance and companionship. At the Agni Institute, understanding, developing and sustaining methods of meditation are supported through discussions, periodic sittings, mentoring and mutual care. In keeping with long-standing tradition, there is no charge for these activities. If you would like to begin with others, <a href="contact.html">get in touch</a> and ask about current sessions, or read more about our <a href="program-meditation.html">meditation program</a> and <a href="benefits-of-meditation.html">what research says about the benefits of meditation</a>.</p>
<p class="note">This guide offers general information and is not medical advice. If you have a mental-health condition or a history of trauma, consider talking with a qualified professional before starting an intensive practice.</p>
""",
faq=[
("How long should a beginner meditate?","Five to ten minutes a day is a good start. Regular short sessions matter more than occasional long ones; extend the time gradually as sitting becomes comfortable."),
("Do I have to sit cross-legged?","No. Sitting upright on a firm chair with your feet flat on the floor works well. What matters is a posture that is alert, stable and comfortable."),
("What if my mind keeps wandering?","That is normal. Each time you notice the mind has wandered and gently return to the breath, you are practicing. Noticing is the skill being trained."),
("Is there a cost to meditate with the Agni Institute?","No. In keeping with long-standing tradition, there is no charge for the Institute's meditation discussions, sittings or mentoring."),
])

L['benefits-of-meditation'] = dict(
title="Benefits of Meditation: What the Research Shows | Agni Institute",
desc="What does research say about the benefits of meditation for stress, anxiety, attention and wellbeing? An evidence-minded overview from the Agni Institute.",
h1="The Benefits of Meditation",
lede="An evidence-minded look at what meditation can and cannot be expected to do, and why the Agni Institute treats it as a tool for understanding consciousness.",
body="""
<h2>An ancient practice under modern study</h2>
<p>Meditation has been practiced for thousands of years in contemplative traditions across Asia and beyond. Over the last several decades it has also become a subject of scientific research, with thousands of studies examining its effects on stress, mood, attention, the body and the brain. The picture that has emerged is encouraging but nuanced: some benefits are supported by reasonably consistent evidence, while others are still being explored.</p>

<h2>Stress, anxiety and mood</h2>
<p>The best-studied benefits relate to stress and emotional wellbeing. A widely cited 2014 systematic review and meta-analysis in <em>JAMA Internal Medicine</em> (Goyal and colleagues) found moderate evidence that mindfulness meditation programs can improve anxiety, depression and pain. Structured programs such as Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) and Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT) have been studied extensively, and MBCT in particular has been investigated as a way to help reduce the risk of relapse in recurrent depression.</p>
<p>These findings do not mean meditation replaces medical or psychological care. They suggest it can be a helpful, low-cost practice that many people find supportive alongside other forms of care.</p>

<h2>Attention and focus</h2>
<p>Meditation is, at its core, attention training. Studies have reported improvements in aspects of attention and the ability to notice when the mind has wandered. Research on the brain's "default mode network", a set of regions active during mind-wandering and self-referential thought, has explored how experienced meditators may engage these networks differently. This is an active field, and results vary between studies, but it fits well with what practitioners describe: a growing capacity to notice thoughts rather than be carried along by them.</p>

<h2>Sleep and the body</h2>
<p>Some studies suggest meditation and mindfulness-based programs may help with sleep quality, particularly where worry and rumination keep people awake. Research has also looked at blood pressure and other physiological measures. The U.S. National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health summarizes the evidence as promising in several areas while noting that many studies are small and that more rigorous research is needed.</p>

<h2>Emotional balance and relationships</h2>
<p>Practices that cultivate kindness and compassion, such as loving-kindness meditation, have been studied for their effects on positive emotion and social connection. Many practitioners report becoming less reactive, more patient and more aware of their own habits of mind. These are harder to measure, but they are among the most commonly described benefits of a steady practice.</p>

<h2>What meditation is not</h2>
<p>An honest account should also name the limits. Meditation is not a cure-all, and effects differ between people and practices. A small number of people report unpleasant experiences, especially during intensive practice or retreats, and anyone with a history of trauma or serious mental-health conditions may benefit from guidance from a qualified professional. Good research, like good practice, avoids overclaiming.</p>

<h2>Meditation as a method of inquiry</h2>
<p>At the Agni Institute, meditation is valued for more than its practical benefits. In the lifestyle promoted by Agni Yoga, it is a central method of self-inquiry and a way to verify insights for oneself. Co-workers exercise disciplines of consciousness that sharpen and extend subtle aspects of awareness, and the Institute's broader work explores how human consciousness itself might be included among the instruments of research.</p>
<p>We believe the combination of Eastern disciplines of consciousness with critical thought and scientific method, when motivated by goodwill, can enhance both. That is why meditation sits alongside our <a href="program-institute-research.html">research programs</a> and <a href="science-of-consciousness.html">our interest in the science of consciousness</a>.</p>

<h2>How to begin</h2>
<p>The benefits described in research generally come from regular practice over weeks and months, not from a single session. If you are new, our <a href="meditation-for-beginners.html">meditation for beginners guide</a> walks through posture, breath and building a daily habit. To practice with others, ask us about periodic sittings and discussions. In keeping with long-standing tradition, there is no charge for these activities.</p>
<p class="note">This page summarizes published research for general information and is not medical advice.</p>
""",
faq=[
("What are the proven benefits of meditation?","The most consistent evidence concerns stress, anxiety, depression and pain, where reviews such as Goyal et al. (2014) found moderate benefits from mindfulness programs. Effects on attention, sleep and physiology are promising but still being studied."),
("How long does it take to notice benefits?","Most studies examine programs lasting several weeks with regular, often daily, practice. Many people notice subtle changes in calm and awareness within a few weeks of consistent practice."),
("Can meditation replace therapy or medication?","No. Meditation can be a helpful complement, but it is not a substitute for professional medical or psychological care."),
("Why does the Agni Institute teach meditation?","The Institute treats meditation as the principal skill for investigating the nature of consciousness, as well as a practice that supports a balanced, ethical daily life."),
])

L['lucid-dreaming'] = dict(
title="Lucid Dreaming and Conscious Dreaming: How It Works | Agni Institute",
desc="What lucid dreaming is, what science knows about it, and practical techniques to become aware while dreaming, from the Agni Institute's Subtle Realm program.",
h1="Lucid Dreaming and Conscious Dreaming",
lede="We spend nearly a third of our lives asleep. Lucid dreaming, becoming aware that you are dreaming while the dream continues, opens that third of life to exploration.",
body="""
<h2>What is lucid dreaming?</h2>
<p>A lucid dream is a dream in which you know you are dreaming. The experience ranges from a brief flash of recognition ("this is a dream") to sustained, clear awareness in which the dreamer can reflect, remember waking intentions and sometimes influence the course of the dream. Many people have had at least one spontaneous lucid dream; with practice, they can become more frequent.</p>

<h2>Exploring the subtle realm</h2>
<p>A primary mission of the Agni Institute is the study of the nature of consciousness, and that includes the nearly one-third of people's lives spent in a state of sleep. In our <a href="program-subtle-realm.html">Subtle Realm program</a>, participants work to understand aspects of consciousness and physiology that shape the experience of dreaming. The program introduces varieties of dream-state experience, ways to affect awareness and capability while dreaming, and involvement in various states of sleep, drawing on a combination of traditional wisdom and current research.</p>
<p>Participants develop skills of conscious activity in the dream state and engage in individual and collective experiments as learning experiences. Discussion forums and related collaboration are part of this work, and contributors can also take part at <a href="https://www.subtlerealm.org" rel="noopener noreferrer">www.subtlerealm.org</a>.</p>

<h2>What science knows</h2>
<p>For a long time lucid dreaming was regarded with skepticism because dream reports cannot be checked directly. That changed when researchers found a way for dreamers to signal from inside the dream. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, Keith Hearne in England and Stephen LaBerge at Stanford independently showed that lucid dreamers in the laboratory could make agreed-upon eye movements during REM sleep, which were recorded on polysomnography. This provided objective evidence that lucidity occurs during genuine sleep.</p>
<p>Since then, researchers have studied the brain activity associated with lucid dreams, techniques for inducing them, and even two-way communication with dreamers. A 2021 study published in <em>Current Biology</em> (Konkoly and colleagues) reported that lucid dreamers in several labs could perceive simple questions and answer them with eye or facial signals while remaining asleep. The field is still young, but it has made dreaming an increasingly accessible subject of careful study.</p>

<h2>Traditional perspectives</h2>
<p>Long before laboratories, contemplative traditions cultivated awareness during sleep. Tibetan Buddhism, for example, preserves practices of dream yoga aimed at recognizing the dream state. The texts of Agni Yoga also speak of the subtle world and of the continuity of consciousness, which is part of why the Institute approaches dreaming as a serious field of inquiry rather than a curiosity.</p>

<h2>Practical techniques</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep a dream journal.</strong> Write down your dreams as soon as you wake, even fragments. Dream recall is the foundation of all dream work, and it improves quickly with practice.</li>
<li><strong>Look for dream signs.</strong> Reviewing your journal reveals recurring people, places and oddities. Recognizing them can trigger lucidity.</li>
<li><strong>Reality checks.</strong> Several times a day, ask sincerely, "Am I dreaming?" and test it, for example by reading a line of text twice. The habit can carry over into dreams.</li>
<li><strong>Set an intention (MILD).</strong> LaBerge's Mnemonic Induction of Lucid Dreams involves rehearsing, as you fall asleep, the intention to recognize that you are dreaming.</li>
<li><strong>Wake back to bed.</strong> Some people wake briefly after several hours of sleep, stay up a short while, then return to sleep with a clear intention, since later sleep cycles are richer in REM.</li>
<li><strong>Meditate.</strong> Daytime attention training supports clearer awareness at night. Our <a href="meditation-for-beginners.html">meditation guide</a> is a good place to start.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Practice responsibly</h2>
<p>Healthy sleep comes first. Techniques that interrupt sleep should be used sparingly, and anyone with a sleep disorder or mental-health condition should seek professional advice. Lucid dreaming is best approached with patience and curiosity rather than pressure.</p>

<h2>Join the exploration</h2>
<p>If you would like to learn about dream-state experience with others and contribute to shared experiments, <a href="contact.html">contact us</a> about the Subtle Realm group. In keeping with long-standing tradition, there is no charge for Institute programs.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Is lucid dreaming real?","Yes. Laboratory studies since the late 1970s have verified lucid dreams by having sleepers signal with pre-arranged eye movements during REM sleep."),
("How can I start lucid dreaming?","Begin with a dream journal to improve recall, add regular reality checks during the day, and set a clear intention to recognize dreams as you fall asleep."),
("Is lucid dreaming safe?","For most people it is a natural experience. Prioritize healthy sleep, use sleep-interrupting techniques sparingly, and seek professional advice if you have a sleep or mental-health condition."),
("What is the Subtle Realm program?","It is the Agni Institute's program exploring consciousness beyond the limits of the physical body, including dreaming and states of sleep, through study and shared experiments."),
])

L['science-of-consciousness'] = dict(
title="The Science of Consciousness: Questions, Theories and Research | Agni Institute",
desc="An accessible introduction to the science of consciousness: the hard problem, leading theories, meditation research and the Agni Institute's approach.",
h1="The Science of Consciousness",
lede="What is consciousness, and can science explain it? An accessible introduction to the central questions and theories, and to how the Agni Institute approaches them.",
body="""
<h2>The oldest question</h2>
<p>Consciousness is the most familiar thing in the world, and one of the least understood. It is simply what it is like to be you: to see colors, feel warmth, think, remember and choose. Philosophers have asked about its nature for millennia. Over the last few decades it has also become an active field of scientific research, bringing together neuroscience, psychology, physics, philosophy and contemplative practice.</p>

<h2>The "hard problem"</h2>
<p>In 1995 the philosopher David Chalmers distinguished between the "easy problems" of consciousness, such as explaining how the brain discriminates stimuli, integrates information and controls behavior, and the "hard problem": why any of this processing is accompanied by subjective experience at all. The easy problems are hard enough, but in principle they can be addressed by describing mechanisms. The hard problem asks why there is something it is like to have those mechanisms. It remains one of the great open questions in science.</p>

<h2>Neural correlates of consciousness</h2>
<p>One productive strategy, championed by Francis Crick and Christof Koch, has been to search for the neural correlates of consciousness: the minimal brain activity that reliably accompanies a specific conscious experience. Researchers compare conditions in which the same stimulus is sometimes consciously perceived and sometimes not, and look for what changes in the brain. This work has taught a great deal about perception, attention and the brain states associated with being awake, dreaming or anesthetized.</p>

<h2>Leading theories</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Global Workspace Theory</strong>, proposed by Bernard Baars and developed by Stanislas Dehaene and others, suggests that information becomes conscious when it is broadcast widely across the brain and becomes available to many processes at once.</li>
<li><strong>Integrated Information Theory</strong>, developed by Giulio Tononi, proposes that consciousness corresponds to how much a system integrates information as a unified whole, and offers a mathematical measure of it.</li>
<li><strong>Higher-order theories</strong> hold that a mental state is conscious when the mind represents itself as being in that state.</li>
<li><strong>Predictive processing</strong> approaches view the brain as constantly generating and updating predictions about the world and the body, with experience shaped by those predictions.</li>
</ul>
<p>These theories make different predictions, and researchers have begun designing "adversarial collaborations" in which proponents agree in advance on experiments that could distinguish between them. No theory has yet won general agreement.</p>

<h2>Contemplative science</h2>
<p>A growing area of research studies meditation and other contemplative practices. Experienced practitioners can offer detailed first-person reports and can deliberately enter particular states of attention, which makes them valuable partners in research. Studies have examined how meditation relates to attention, emotion regulation and networks such as the brain's default mode network. Sleep and dreaming, including <a href="lucid-dreaming.html">lucid dreaming</a>, offer another window, since consciousness changes dramatically across a single night.</p>

<h2>The Agni Institute's approach</h2>
<p>At the Agni Institute, our mission is to serve the common good by awakening and expanding human consciousness. We believe the combination of Eastern and Western methods, meaning disciplines of consciousness, critical thought and scientific methodology, when motivated by goodwill, is capable of enhancing both.</p>
<p>Contained in the texts of Agni Yoga are hundreds of suggestions regarding research into natural law and the nature of consciousness. In our <a href="program-institute-research.html">Institute Research</a> program, co-workers collate these suggestions and write research and test protocols, using laboratory space and equipment for benchwork such as building fixtures and circuits for measurement and data collection. Through <a href="program-collaborative-research.html">Collaborative Research</a>, participants at three levels, from enthusiast to citizen scientist to professional or academic investigator, follow defined methods and share methods, data and findings.</p>
<p>We hold that unconventional questions deserve neither automatic acceptance nor automatic dismissal, but careful investigation using appropriate methods, clear standards of evidence and intellectual openness. Alongside research, <a href="program-meditation.html">meditation</a> is practiced as a way to include human consciousness itself among the instruments of inquiry.</p>

<h2>Get involved</h2>
<p>Whether you are curious, a citizen scientist or a trained researcher, there are ways to take part. <a href="contact.html">Contact us</a> to ask about current projects, or read about <a href="get-involved.html">how to get involved</a>.</p>
""",
faq=[
("What is the hard problem of consciousness?","Coined by philosopher David Chalmers in 1995, it is the question of why physical processing in the brain is accompanied by subjective experience at all, rather than happening without any inner experience."),
("What are the main scientific theories of consciousness?","Prominent theories include Global Workspace Theory, Integrated Information Theory, higher-order theories and predictive processing. None has yet achieved general agreement."),
("How does meditation relate to consciousness research?","Meditators can provide detailed first-person reports and deliberately enter particular attentional states, making contemplative practice a valuable method in consciousness research."),
("Can I take part in the Agni Institute's research?","Yes. Collaborative Research is open at three levels: enthusiast, citizen scientist, and professional or academic investigator. Contact us to learn about current projects."),
])

L['helena-roerich'] = dict(
title="Helena Roerich: Life, Agni Yoga and Legacy | Agni Institute",
desc="Who was Helena Roerich? Her life, travels, the Agni Yoga (Living Ethics) teachings recorded between 1924 and 1938, and her lasting legacy.",
h1="Helena Roerich and the Agni Yoga Teachings",
lede="Philosopher, writer and translator, Helena Roerich (1879–1955) recorded the Agni Yoga series, the Teaching of Living Ethics that inspires the Agni Institute's work.",
body="""
<h2>Early life</h2>
<p>Helena Ivanovna Roerich (née Shaposhnikova; in Russian, Elena Rerikh) was born in 1879 in St. Petersburg, Russia. She received a broad education, showing particular gifts in music and a deep interest in philosophy and religious thought. In 1901 she married the painter, archaeologist and writer Nicholas Roerich. They had two sons: George (Yuri), who became a distinguished Tibetologist and orientalist, and Svetoslav, who became a painter.</p>

<h2>Travels and the Central Asian expedition</h2>
<p>After leaving Russia, the Roerich family lived in Europe and then, from 1920, in the United States, where Nicholas Roerich's art and cultural work drew wide attention. In the mid-1920s the family undertook a long expedition through Central Asia, crossing regions of India, Tibet, Mongolia and western China. Helena took part throughout, one of the few women to travel such routes at the time. In 1928 the family settled in the Kullu Valley in the Indian Himalayas, where they founded the Urusvati Himalayan Research Institute to study the region's natural history, medicine, languages and culture.</p>

<h2>The Agni Yoga series</h2>
<p>Agni Yoga is the name given to a collection of writings recorded by Helena Roerich. These were essentially dictated to her between 1924 and 1938 by Eastern teachers. The series covers philosophy, ethics, spirituality and the evolution of human consciousness, and is sometimes called the Teaching of Living Ethics. It forms a synthesis of tenets, understanding and disciplines from Buddhist and Hindu traditions, with an emphasis on the transformation of consciousness, given with the intention of informing and mentoring humanity in a new era.</p>
<p>The name draws on Agni, the god of fire found in the Rig Veda, who represents physical, vital and spiritual fire within living beings. In this context Agni Yoga is intended to bring about an awakening and mastery of the fires of illumination of consciousness. The books in the series include <em>Leaves of Morya's Garden</em> (I and II), <em>New Era Community</em>, <em>Agni Yoga</em>, <em>Infinity</em> (I and II), <em>Hierarchy</em>, <em>Heart</em>, <em>Fiery World</em> (I–III), <em>Aum</em>, <em>Brotherhood</em> and <em>Supermundane</em>. You can download them free on our <a href="books-of-agni-yoga.html">Books of Agni Yoga</a> page.</p>

<h2>Other writings and translations</h2>
<p>Helena Roerich was also a prolific correspondent and translator. Her translation of Helena Blavatsky's <em>The Secret Doctrine</em> into Russian made that work available to a new readership. She wrote <em>Foundations of Buddhism</em>, published under the pseudonym Natalie Rokotova, and <em>On Eastern Crossroads</em>, published under the name Josephine Saint-Hilaire. Selections from her extensive correspondence were later published as the <em>Letters of Helena Roerich</em>, in which she answers questions from students about the practical application of the Teaching in daily life.</p>

<h2>Later years and legacy</h2>
<p>Nicholas Roerich died in the Kullu Valley in 1947. Helena later moved to Kalimpong in the eastern Himalayas, where she continued her writing and correspondence until her death in 1955. Her work, and that of the Roerich family, is preserved and studied by museums, societies and readers around the world, and the Agni Yoga books continue to be read in many languages.</p>
<p>Her central message is practical: that spiritual growth must be expressed in daily life, through labor, beauty, cooperation and the refinement of thought and heart. That emphasis on lived ethics, joined to an open spirit of inquiry, is what draws many readers to the Teaching today.</p>

<h2>Helena Roerich and the Agni Institute</h2>
<p>The Agni Institute takes its inspiration from the writings and teachings of Agni Yoga. We support study of the texts through dialogues and mentoring, practice meditation as a method of self-inquiry, and pursue research suggested in the books. The Institute is not officially affiliated with any organization. The texts are made available for free download by the generosity of their copyright holders, on the condition that they are not modified or used commercially.</p>
<p>Read more <a href="about-agni-yoga.html">about Agni Yoga</a>, explore our <a href="program-studies.html">Studies in Agni Yoga</a>, or <a href="contact.html">contact us</a> to join a study group.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Who was Helena Roerich?","Helena Roerich (1879–1955) was a Russian philosopher, writer and translator who recorded the Agni Yoga series and traveled widely in Central Asia with her husband, the painter Nicholas Roerich."),
("What is Agni Yoga?","Agni Yoga, also called the Teaching of Living Ethics, is a series of books recorded by Helena Roerich between 1924 and 1938 covering philosophy, ethics, spirituality and the evolution of consciousness."),
("Where can I read the Agni Yoga books?","The books can be downloaded free in PDF and ePub on the Agni Institute's Books of Agni Yoga page, under the copyright holders' conditions of no modification and no commercial use."),
("Is the Agni Institute affiliated with the Roerich organizations?","No. The Institute takes inspiration from the Agni Yoga teachings but is not officially affiliated with any organization."),
])
